abstract:
In this paper we study non-singular solutions of Ricci flow on a closed manifold of dimension at least 4. Amongst others we prove that, if M is a closed 4-manifold on which the normalized Ricci flow exists for all time t>0 with uniformly bounded sectional curvature, then the Euler characteristic $\chi (M)\ge 0$. Moreover, the 4-manifold satisfies one of the following 
oindent (i) M is a shrinking Ricci solition; 
oindent (ii) M admits a positive rank F-structure; 
oindent (iii) the Hitchin-Thorpe type inequality holds 2\chi (M)\ge 3|	au(M)| where $\chi (M)$ (resp. $	au(M)$) is the Euler characteristic (resp. signature) of M.

We consider zero-range processes in ${\mathbb{Z}}^d$ with site dependent jump rates. The rate for a particle jump from site $x$ to $y$ in ${\mathbb{Z}}^d$ is given by $\lambda_xg(k)p(y-x)$, where $p(\cdot)$ is a probability in ${\mathbb{Z}}^d$, $g(k)$ is a bounded nondecreasing function of the number $k$ of particles in $x$ and $\lambda =\{\lambda_x\}$ is a collection of i.i.d. random variables with values in $(c,1]$, for some $c>0$. For almost every realization of the environment $\lambda$ the zero-range process has product invariant measures $\{{
u_{\lambda, v}}:0\le v\le c\}$ parametrized by $v$, the average total jump rate from any given site. The density of a measure, defined by the asymptotic average number of particles per site, is an increasing function of $v$. There exists a product invariant measure ${
u _{\lambda, c}}$, with maximal density. Let $\mu$ be a probability measure concentrating mass on configurations whose number of particles at site $x$ grows less than exponentially with $\|x\|$. Denoting by $S_{\lambda}(t)$ the semigroup of the process, we prove that all weak limits of $\{\mu S_{\lambda}(t),t\ge 0\}$ as $t	o \infty$ are dominated, in the natural partial order, by ${
u_{\lambda, c}}$. In particular, if $\mu$ dominates ${
u _{\lambda, c}}$, then $\mu S_{\lambda}(t)$ converges to ${
u_{\lambda, c}}$. The result is particularly striking when the maximal density is finite and the initial measure has a density above the maximal.
